    "created_at": "2021-07-04T14:30:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-07-04T14:30:28Z",
    "body": "It may not be a satisfying answer, but the reason I decided to use \"Golang\" and\"Vlang\" is to make our docs indexable and searchable. Search providers will often lower the confidence score of search terms that are very short because they have multiple uses.\r\n\r\nFor instance, searching \"Starship go\" on a search engine will produce results about SpaceX and their starship \"going\" to mars. Not to say that we can't compete with those indexed result, but Go developers have long since gotten used to using the \"golang\" search term to disambiguate between the programming language, the conjugated \"going\" verb, and the boardgame of \"Go\".\r\n\r\nSimilarly, it makes it easy for us to search our own codebase for couplings with the golang module. Searching \"go\" brings up any reference to \"Car**go**\".\r\n\r\nI'd be open to changing it though. Just sharing the reasoning behind why it is this way today. 😊",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
